# Render path reads memory

**Anything a component needs while rendering must already be in memory.** If answering a question
requires I/O, answer it at **write** time and store the answer. Never re-derive it at **read** time.

Written 2026-08-25 after meal thumbs (prompt116) were pulled. One photo made the whole dashboard
feel slow — not because a JPEG is heavy, but because two paths asked about it constantly.

## The 60 fps test

Before adding state or an effect to anything on a screen, ask: **if this re-rendered 60 times a
second, what would it touch?**

| Free — do this | Costly — never on a render path |
|---|---|
| Fields already on loaded objects (`entry.photoId`) | `FileSystem.getInfoAsync` / `readDirectoryAsync` |
| `Boolean(...)`, arithmetic, string compare | `AsyncStorage.getItem` / `multiGet` / `getAllKeys` |
| `<Image source={{ uri: 'file://…' }} />` — native decode | `readAsStringAsync` (any encoding) |
| A path or id **string** | `fetch`, or a `data:` URI |

An `await` inside a `useEffect` keyed on a prop that changes identity every render is the shape to
watch for. It fires far more than it looks like it will, and each pass usually calls `setState`,
which schedules another render.

## Write time, not read time

The failure was asking a question whose answer was already known:

```ts
// wrong — one disk stat per meal, on every chart refresh
useEffect(() => {
  void resolveMealPhotoIds(foodEntries).then(setMealPhotoIds); // getInfoAsync per meal
}, [foodEntries]);

// right — the pointer written at save time IS the answer
const hasPhoto = Boolean(entry.photoId);
```

Prefer a stale flag over a fresh `stat`. A mark that points at a purged file is cosmetic and
self-corrects; handle it with `onError` on the `<Image>` in the already-open card, where it is free.
I/O in a render path drops frames every single time.

## Lazy is not the goal — the right timing is

"Load it lazily" is not the fix by itself. v1 *was* lazy about the bytes and still stalled,
because it was lazy about a question that had a permanent answer.

| Work | Timing | Why |
|---|---|---|
| Expensive one-time transform (resize, hash, parse) | **Eager, once**, at write | The user is already waiting; every later read is free |
| "Does this record have X?" | **Pre-answered** — a field written at the same time | A lazy check here repeats per render forever |
| Decoding / fetching the payload | **Lazy**, when the view that needs it mounts | Only the window that demands it pays |
| Sending it anywhere | **Lazy**, on an explicit tap | Never on a timer, foreground, or poll |

Gate by **tree structure, not a boolean prop.** If only the open card imports `<Image>`, there
is nothing to flag off — closed means absent. A `loadPhoto={false}` prop means the dashboard
knows about photos, and the next person adds a check beside it.

Unmounting a modal is right; unmounting a strip the user toggles constantly is not (see the
mount-once pattern in `useKeepMountedExpand`). Cheap-to-build and rarely-opened unmounts;
expensive native view trees stay mounted and hide.

## Corollary — bytes never enter JS

JS is one thread, shared by rendering, gestures, and every service. Work proportional to file size
on that thread starves the UI even when nothing is displayed.

- **No base64 as storage.** 33% larger, and `<Image>` cannot read it without pulling the string
 through JS. Base64 is an escape hatch for text-only channels, not a format.
- **Never inline file bytes in a snapshot.** `buildClinicExport` dumps AsyncStorage wholesale, then
 `JSON.stringify` + `pako.deflate` the result — joining that payload means re-serializing a year of
 unrelated data on every auto sync.
- **Upload files natively:** `FileSystem.uploadAsync(url, fileUri, { uploadType: BINARY_CONTENT })`.
 Native code streams the socket; JS gets `{ status }`.
- **Do not gzip JPEG or PDF.** Already compressed. Resize + JPEG quality at capture is the only
 compression that pays.
- Files, not SQLite blobs, for anything an image view renders — the filesystem is the only store the
 native decoder can read without JS seeing a byte.

## Mitigations are a smell

Debounce, TTL cache, `InteractionManager`, `setTimeout(0)`, deferred launch — each of these hides
the problem well enough to pass review. If a path needs them to feel acceptable, the work does not
belong on that path. Move it, don't tune it.

## Precedent

- **prompt116 pulled** — `resolveMealPhotoIds` statted disk per meal per chart refresh, and
 `injectMealPhotosSidecar` put base64 thumbs inside the gzipped clinic snapshot that runs on launch,
 foreground, and clinic poll. Both were mitigated with caches and yields before being removed.
- **A request to raise a payload cap is the tripwire.** be-53 needed sync at 32 MB and cloud backup
 at 40 MB. That was not the fix — it was file bytes announcing they had reached the JSON.
- `injectLabPdfsSidecar` still runs inside `buildClinicExport`. Accepted at a handful of small PDFs
 (prompt117); the same chain, so do not grow it.

## Checklist

- [ ] Nothing on a render path touches disk, network, or AsyncStorage?
- [ ] Every derived flag stored at write time, not re-derived per render?
- [ ] Heavy payload gated by which component is mounted, not by a boolean prop threaded down?
- [ ] One-time transforms done eagerly at write, not lazily on each view?
- [ ] No effect awaiting I/O keyed on a prop with unstable identity?
- [ ] Image bytes stay in files and native upload — no base64, no `data:` URI, no snapshot inline?
- [ ] No debounce/yield/TTL added to make a hot path tolerable?
- [ ] No payload cap raised to fit file bytes?
